 Corvus brachyrhynchos, the American crow, can remember human faces for at least five years and will scold specific individuals on sight. We’ll keep the routine clean and quiet.
Octavio: …Right. So slow the rhythm, hold the catch, then resume. How long is an eye-beat?
Pippa: Roughly a quarter-second. Crows tap their bills against metal at about two taps per second when caching food;

 Fine, skip the tape. Corvus corax—the common raven—can remember human faces for years, so if you keep your eye on the seam the same way a raven tracks a reliable feeder, you’ll spot the drift before it becomes a problem.
Nerys: That’s… oddly specific. Are you saying I should watch the fabric like a hawk?
Lucien: Exactly. Ravens cache food in thousands of spots and still find it months later;
